92186065192 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065193 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065193 #edb/ref 1061 3524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065193 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e5df8da7-4ba5-5203-9fe1-9d3c0405217c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065193 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065194 #edb/ref 1061 3526 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065194 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f852560e-1702-515d-ab85-f06ccdd54d76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065194 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065337 #edb/ref 1064 "The **quotient rule for exponents** states that when we divide two exponents that have the same base, we take the difference of the exponents in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m}=a^{n-m}\n$$\n\nFor example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}^{5}}{{a}^{2}} & = \\\\ {a}^{(5-2)} & = \\\\ {a}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo see why the quotient rule is true, let's take the above example and work it out again, the long way.\n\nFor the two terms $a^5$ and $a^2$ we have $a^5 = a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a$ and $a^2 = a \\cdot a.$ If we now substitute these two expressions into $\\dfrac{a^5}{a^2},$ we get the same result:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac{a^{5}}{a^2}&=\\\n\\dfrac {a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a} {a \\cdot a}&=\\\n\\dfrac {\\cancel{a} \\cdot \\cancel{a} \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a} {\\cancel{a} \\cdot \\cancel{a} }&=\\\na \\cdot a \\cdot a &=\\\n a^3\n}\n$$\n\nThe quotient rule is just a shortcut for the process above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065337 #edb/ref 1065 1478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065337 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5ce1b58b-8395-5986-99e6-cfd10bbca653" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065337 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540519 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186541177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540519